Title: a forced ftsStorage overrides the layout recorded in the file
Created: 2026-09-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-02T18:52Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

Found by the review of the fork's merge commit `c031478` ("Merge tranche 3
and the v2 content schema into one tree"), alongside the P0 of the deposed
conductor (fixed separately) and 0595, 0596. Pre-existing: the merge did not
introduce it, it inherited it from the content-schema half.

`resolveFtsStorage` is meant to enforce one rule, and its own docstring
states it in as many words:

> The recorded value wins over the probe, always. A runtime that gained
> `contentless_delete` since the file was written must still speak the
> delete protocol the existing rows are in; re-probing on every open would
> switch protocols under live data.

The function does not do that. `LedgerOpenOptions.ftsStorage` is consulted
*first*, ahead of the recorded value:

```ts
function resolveFtsStorage(db: Database, forced?: FtsStorage): FtsStorage {
  if (forced) return forced;                       // ← before the record
  const recorded = readRecordedFtsStorage(db);
  if (recorded) return recorded;
  ...
}
```

and `createSchema` then re-stamps `ledger_meta.ftsStorage` with
`INSERT OR REPLACE`, so the override is not scoped to the open that asked
for it: it rewrites the file's recorded layout permanently.

The consequence is exactly the one the docstring exists to prevent — the
delete protocol switched under live rows — and it is silent in both
directions. `unindexText` on an `external` ledger reads the shadow row from
`fts_content`, finds nothing (a contentless file has no shadow rows), and
returns:

```ts
const row = ... .get(pid);
if (!row) return;     // a retirement that retires nothing
```

so a retired passage keeps matching. The reverse pairing (an `external`
file forced to `contentless`) deletes the `fts` row without handing back
the text FTS5 indexed, which is the corruption the same docstring names.

Nothing in the shipped server passes the option today — `openConductorLedger`
does not — so this is a trap rather than a live defect: it is the option's
only purpose to be passed, and the tests that pass it are what a future
caller will copy.

## Reproduction

Measured on `conductor-integration` at `c031478`, node 22.22, vitest 2.1.9,
a real file on disk (not `:memory:`):

```ts
const created = Ledger.open(path);              // → contentless on this runtime
created.indexText(1, { title: 'sphinx of black quartz' });
created.searchFts('sphinx');                    // → [ 1 ]
created.close();

const forced = Ledger.open(path, undefined, { ftsStorage: 'external' });
forced.ftsStorage;                              // → 'external'
forced.unindexText(1);                           // silent no-op
forced.searchFts('sphinx');                     // → [ 1 ]   ← still indexed
forced.close();

const later = Ledger.open(path);                 // no option at all
later.ftsStorage;                                // → 'external'  ← re-stamped
later.searchFts('sphinx');                       // → [ 1 ]
```

Printed output of the run:

```
created as: contentless
search after index: [ 1 ]
reopened as: external
search after unindex: [ 1 ]
reopened with NO option: external
search from the plain reopen: [ 1 ]
```

The third block is the part that makes this worth a ticket rather than a
comment: one open with the option poisons every later open without it.

## Relevant files

- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/ledger.ts` — `resolveFtsStorage`, the
  ordering; `Ledger.open`, which calls it; `createSchema`, the
  `INSERT OR REPLACE` that re-stamps `ledger_meta.ftsStorage`; `indexText` /
  `unindexText`, the two protocols.
- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/store.ts` — `openConductorLedger`, the
  one production caller, which passes no option.

## Actions

1. Decide what `LedgerOpenOptions.ftsStorage` is *for*, since the answer
   settles the fix. Two candidates:
   - **A test seam for a file being created.** Then the recorded value must
     win and the option only steers the probe: move the `forced` check below
     `readRecordedFtsStorage`, and the trap closes with one moved line.
   - **A deliberate override.** Then it is a migration, not an open option,
     and it owes a rebuild of the `fts` table into the requested layout
     rather than a re-stamp over rows in the other one.
   A is what the docstring already promises and what every current caller
   wants; B is a feature nobody has asked for.
2. Whichever is chosen, make the disagreement loud rather than silent: an
   open that is handed a layout the file contradicts should refuse
   (`LedgerVersionError` is the existing precedent for "this file is not
   what you think it is") instead of proceeding under the wrong protocol.

## Test

- Red first: create a `contentless` file, index a passage, reopen it with
  `{ ftsStorage: 'external' }`, and assert the reopened ledger reports
  `contentless` — the recorded value winning. Against the code as it stands
  it reports `external`.
- The retirement is the observable half, and cheaper to trust than a
  property of a string: after `unindexText`, `searchFts` must not return the
  passage. Today it does.
- The stamp: a plain reopen after the forced one still reports the layout
  the file was created in.

## Verification

- [ ] The recorded layout wins over the option, or the mismatch refuses
- [ ] `unindexText` retires a passage on every path a caller can reach
- [ ] One open with the option cannot change what a later open without it sees

## Invariants

- No change to which layout a *new* file is created in: the probe, and the
  runtime's actual `contentless_delete` support, still decide that.
- Upstream tests green.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The layout a file was written in is the layout it is read in, merged
      on a fork branch, tests green
